i'm sarah schneiderman i'm assistant  professor of anthropology and south  asian studies at yale and i work in  nepal himalayan parts of india and also  in the tibetan autonomous region of  china i'm a socio-cultural  anthropologist  i've been going to the himalayan region  for nearly 20 years now i went for the  first time in 1994 as a college student  and i was studying anthropology and  religious studies and was just  fascinated by the diversity of cultural  and religious practices and the way that  they intermingled and the dynamic nature  of social life in the region i've been  at yale for nearly two years now and i  teach in anthropology as well as in  south asian studies i've been involved  in building up the south asian studies  program here particularly in my current  role as director of undergraduate  studies for south asian studies  and i've also been involved with the  yale himalaya initiative since its  inception i've been serving as faculty  coordinator and just doing a range of  playing a range of different roles to  help bring speakers into campus and to  really build up the profile of himalayan  studies at yale i first went to nepal as  a college student as i mentioned and i  was very interested in studying religion  particularly buddhism and hinduism  and as i began to become more interested  in those topics and began to spend time  in different parts of the country living  mostly in rural village areas i started  to understand that actually most people  and i would say that anecdotally most  people in nepal  actually have some sense of one of the  other countries nearby have either spent  time themselves working or in education  in india or perhaps china  particularly the tibetan autonomous  region many people are spend time there  for trade or other kinds of  livelihood occupations and so i became  increasingly aware of the fact that even  though  looking from a kind of american or  western perspective we often tend to  think of the himalayas as a remote and  isolated location actually many of the  people who i was working with in these  remarkable  locations were in fact very cosmopolitan  and were spending time traveling between  multiple different countries doing a  whole range of different things so i  actually followed the people who i began  working with in nepal  to their work sites particularly in  himalayan parts of india and darjeeling  and sikhim and also into the tibetan  autonomous region of china across the  border to the north and what i found was  as i was trying to understand  livelihoods and ethnic identity for  people who were living in rural nepal  that it was absolutely impossible to  understand what they were doing and why  they were doing what they were doing  without also understanding something  about the broader regional configuration  and their own experiences of mobility  across borders  mobility across borders in the himalayas  is actually a very long-standing feature  of livelihoods for many many people  these days we often tend to think of  cross-border migration or mobility as a  feature or a consequence of  globalization but i would suggest that  actually for this part of the world  that's not the case at all and in fact  many  historical trade routes depended on  mobility across what we would now see as  nation-state borders whether between  nepal and historical tibet or between  nepal and india also looking further  east for instance between india and  burma burma and china and then in the  west of course between nepal india  kashmir pakistan i'm using the  contemporary  nation-state names but the point is that  historically many people have been very  very mobile across this broad region and  livelihoods have often depended on a  combination of subsistence agriculture  which is of course necessarily rooted in  one place  but that's complemented by different  kinds of trade or wage labor which in  fact depend upon mobility my doctoral  research which i'm now turning into a  book focuses on these processes of  cross-border circular migration for one  ethnic community called the thang me and  i look at how for the thang me  cross-border circular migration is  actually intimately tied up with  processes of ethnic identity formation  so usually ethnicity is talked about  within the framework of one nation state  or it's talked about as somehow being  deterioratorialized or transnational in  this day and age but i argue instead  that actually the experience of being in  different states and the different state  policies that people encounter has  significant effects on the way that they  conceptualize and also enact and express  ethnic identity and their very sense of  themselves  it's been very important to me to  maintain an open and continuous dialogue  with the people with whom i work  particularly members of the thang me  community in relation to my doctoral  work um  and to make sure that what i'm writing  about them actually reflects  as accurately as possible what they  think about themselves now this is a  challenging proposition because they  don't all think the same things about  themselves and this is one of the  large points that i try to make in my  book that just because people share an  ethnic identity doesn't mean that they  all think that ethnic identity is  produced in the same way or is  meaningful in the same way so one of the  big issues i have to deal with is a very  diverse community actually spread across  several different countries  and with very different kinds of  educational and class  positions so what you end up with is a  situation in which certain people would  like to control the types of things i  write and ask me to write one thing  instead of another and my position has  always been well i am actually trying to  represent the community as a whole and  that requires sometimes having competing  and contradictory voices in there when  we think about the himalayas as a unit  of analysis i think what's really  important is to on the one hand  recognize the constituent nation states  that make up the himalayan region india  and nepal bhutan china pakistan  probably others that i've missed out  so to recognize  the specificity of those nation-states  the kinds of policies that they apply  the effects that those have both on  people and on the natural environment  but at the same time to look at movement  across those boundaries and to  understand what connects them so i think  one of the most promising  aspects of the looking at the himalayas  as a focus of as a new kind of area  studies is in fact to do both of those  things at once
 

